# Playbook comments are teaching material, not a lab notebook

**The reader is a network engineering student who knows networking and does
not know Ansible.** They are reading the playbook to understand what each task
does and why it is there. Write every comment for that person.

This applies to all playbooks in this repository, and is enforced hardest in
`ansible-automation/02_data_center/`.

## What a comment must do

- **Say what the task does, in plain language.** "Ask each switch for its
  serial number over SSH" beats "invoke nxos_facts gather_subset".
- **Explain the Ansible mechanism when it is not obvious.** A student has not
  met `register`, `loop`, `when`, `until`/`retries`, `block`/`rescue`,
  `set_fact`, `delegate_to` or `run_once` before. One short clause is enough:
  "`register` saves the result so the next task can read it."
- **Keep the fact that saves someone a day.** An exact error string, an API
  path, a version number, a required header, a documented limit. These are the
  reason the comment exists.
- **Name the file a value comes from**, so the student knows where to edit.

## What a comment must not do

- **No theatrics.** No emphatic capitals for drama (`WHY THIS IS NOT IN THE
  MODEL`, `THE ONE PRECONDITION THAT MATTERS`), no "the trap is", no "this
  cost real time to find", no "read this slowly", no rhetorical questions, no
  suspense.
- **No debugging diary.** Which theories were wrong, what was believed on
  which date, and what a past run proved are project history. They belong in
  the README and the release notes, not above a task.
- **No source-code archaeology.** Module internals and upstream line numbers
  (`dcnm_vrf.py lines 5126-5127`) mean nothing to a student and go stale. Keep
  the observable behaviour and the error string; put the line numbers in the
  README.
- **No arguing with the reader.** Not "do NOT re-run hoping for a different
  answer". State the condition plainly: "Re-running does not help; the link
  must exist first."
- **No restating the task name.** If `- name:` already says it, the comment
  adds nothing.

## Form

- Header comment per playbook: what it builds, what must run first, how to run
  it, and the useful `-e` overrides. A short paragraph and a short list, not an
  essay.
- Inline comments sit above the task and run a few lines at most. Prefer a
  clear `- name:` over a comment explaining an unclear one.
- Plain sentences and normal capitalisation. Use bold sparingly or not at all;
  these are code comments.
- `ansible-automation/02_data_center/` is **pure ASCII**. No em dashes, no
  curly quotes, no arrows. Check with `rg -n '[^\x00-\x7F]' <path>`, never with
  BSD `grep`.

## The same rules apply to anything the student reads on screen

A comment is read by whoever opens the file. A `msg:` is read by everyone who
runs the playbook, which is more people. So `assert` messages (`fail_msg`,
`success_msg`), `fail` and `debug` messages, and `- name:` task names are held
to this standard too, and in a failure path they matter more than the comments.

A good failure message says what is wrong, where, and what to do next:

```yaml
fail_msg: >-
  Nexus Dashboard is not offering {{ dc_vrf_lite_border_leaf }} a VRF-Lite
  extension, so the attachment cannot be made. That means no VRF-Lite
  inter-fabric link terminates on {{ dc_vrf_lite_interface }}. To see what
  the controller does hold, run:
  ansible-playbook playbooks/diagnose_vrf_lite.yml
```

Keep the controller's own error string when quoting it helps someone search
for it. Drop the upstream line numbers, the dates, the disproved theories and
the instructions not to try something. "Re-running does not help" is a fact a
student can use; "Do NOT re-run hoping for a different answer" is scolding.

## Where the long story goes instead

Depth is not the enemy; misplaced depth is. The README is the right home for
forensic detail, disproved theories, captured payloads and module internals,
and it is where a comment should point when a student wants more:

```yaml
# The controller only offers this extension where a VRF-Lite inter-fabric
# connection already exists, so the stage checks for one before writing.
# Background: "What stage 05 needs from the controller" in the README.
```

Trimming a comment is only safe once the detail exists somewhere else. Confirm
it is in the README before deleting it from a playbook.
